WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.483 --> 00:00:03.733
(pleasant piano music)

00:00:06.395 --> 00:00:07.987
- [Narrator] California's iconic sequoias

00:00:07.987 --> 00:00:10.648
are the largest trees on Earth.

00:00:10.648 --> 00:00:13.049
These giants have lived
for thousands of years,

00:00:13.049 --> 00:00:17.717
surviving war, famine and
the rise of globalization,

00:00:17.717 --> 00:00:21.884
but California's ongoing
drought could mean disaster.

00:00:23.658 --> 00:00:26.278
These enormous trees are already
showing signs of weakness

00:00:26.278 --> 00:00:27.778
never seen before.

00:00:29.752 --> 00:00:31.800
Anthony Ambrose and a team of researchers

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:33.500
are heading into the forest

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:36.293
to climb hundreds of feet in the air

00:00:36.293 --> 00:00:39.324
and see what can be done to
save these magnificent trees.

00:00:39.324 --> 00:00:42.074
(dramatic music)

00:00:51.227 --> 00:00:55.849
- Our research entails climbing
the giant sequoia trees

00:00:55.849 --> 00:00:58.718
and measuring their water status,

00:00:58.718 --> 00:01:00.928
basically an index of
how stressed they are

00:01:00.928 --> 00:01:03.261
relative to the environment.

00:01:04.658 --> 00:01:06.616
The water status of all plants

00:01:06.616 --> 00:01:10.020
is kind of like the equivalent
of blood pressure in a human.

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:14.634
It's just a basic measure
of how much water they have

00:01:14.634 --> 00:01:18.691
available to them to
continue to function and grow

00:01:18.691 --> 00:01:20.696
so we clip little shoots of foliage

00:01:20.696 --> 00:01:24.863
and we put the little piece
of foliage leaf end down

00:01:25.926 --> 00:01:28.582
into this pressurized chamber

00:01:28.582 --> 00:01:30.219
and the amount of
pressure it takes to push

00:01:30.219 --> 00:01:31.667
the water back out of the stem

00:01:31.667 --> 00:01:34.716
is equal to the amount
of tension it was under

00:01:34.716 --> 00:01:38.290
when you cut that piece of foliage.

00:01:38.290 --> 00:01:41.528
When water availability declines,
such as during a drought,

00:01:41.528 --> 00:01:45.195
that creates a lot more
tension in the tree.

00:01:46.857 --> 00:01:48.886
When the tension becomes too great,

00:01:48.886 --> 00:01:52.356
then tiny little air
bubbles form in the wood

00:01:52.356 --> 00:01:53.970
and when it gets bad enough

00:01:53.970 --> 00:01:57.148
then the plant suffers and starts to die.

00:01:57.148 --> 00:02:00.231
(gentle piano music)

00:02:04.875 --> 00:02:07.018
There's a couple reasons
why we need to climb

00:02:07.018 --> 00:02:09.268
to the tops of these trees.

00:02:11.732 --> 00:02:13.282
As water moves up the tree,

00:02:13.282 --> 00:02:15.276
it's fighting gravity and friction

00:02:15.276 --> 00:02:17.158
and the top of the tree is gonna be

00:02:17.158 --> 00:02:19.541
the most stressed part of the tree.

00:02:19.541 --> 00:02:21.007
So, in order to get a really good idea

00:02:21.007 --> 00:02:23.258
of how stressed they are,

00:02:23.258 --> 00:02:26.258
we need to go to the very tippy top.

00:02:29.931 --> 00:02:31.380
It's a real humbling experience

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:32.931
because you're up in the crown

00:02:32.931 --> 00:02:34.835
of these massive, ancient things

00:02:34.835 --> 00:02:37.252
and they make you feel small.

00:02:39.811 --> 00:02:42.152
I'm at the top of a very
large, giant sequoia tree

00:02:42.152 --> 00:02:44.812
waiting for the sunrise
'cause it's really,

00:02:44.812 --> 00:02:47.330
really pretty this morning.

00:02:47.330 --> 00:02:49.389
It's important to realize and remember

00:02:49.389 --> 00:02:52.351
that these trees have been
living for thousands of years

00:02:52.351 --> 00:02:54.992
and they've dealt with
pretty severe events,

00:02:54.992 --> 00:02:56.712
fires and droughts in the past,

00:02:56.712 --> 00:02:58.517
and they're amazingly resilient.

00:02:58.517 --> 00:03:00.529
One of the things that
really is remarkable

00:03:00.529 --> 00:03:02.243
about these trees is their ability

00:03:02.243 --> 00:03:05.467
to recover from drought-type events.

00:03:05.467 --> 00:03:07.152
The question is, as conditions change,

00:03:07.152 --> 00:03:09.967
as temperatures continue
to increase globally,

00:03:09.967 --> 00:03:12.482
are conditions gonna change to a point

00:03:12.482 --> 00:03:14.205
where they may reach some threshold

00:03:14.205 --> 00:03:16.143
where they're not able to recover

00:03:16.143 --> 00:03:17.694
from these type of events.

00:03:17.694 --> 00:03:21.115
(dramatic music)

00:03:21.115 --> 00:03:24.014
It's an absolutely
amazing, unique experience

00:03:24.014 --> 00:03:26.336
to be able to climb into these trees.

00:03:26.336 --> 00:03:27.560
We want to preserve them.

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:29.607
We want them to be here and we want them

00:03:29.607 --> 00:03:31.607
to be healthy and happy.

00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:36.227
(dinging)

